.TH "SKARN-CHECK" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.36.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-check \- Scan AI sessions for leaked credentials and attack patterns
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBcheck\fR [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Scan AI sessions for leaked credentials and attack patterns.
.PP
Control the security scan: which rules run, the scan window, the output
format, and CI gating. check needs a license and exits 7 before scanning
without a usable one. The free license is issued at https://getskarn.com/free
after an email confirmation, and skarn verifies it offline. Only
\-\-audit\-verify is exempt. The policy file (\-\-policy), the org baseline
directory that \-\-baseline unions and \-\-baseline\-merge consolidates, and
the hash\-chained audit log (\-\-audit\-log) are Pro capabilities of check:
check reads the policy file and the org baseline directory, and it reads the
audit log's chain head before appending this scan's record. When it decides a
verdict, skarn guard consults only the personal accepted\-findings file. The
four permissive keyword\-assignment rules (generic\-api\-key,
inline\-credential\-assignment, dotenv\-contents\-leaked and
export\-secret\-env) report a match on a line that starts with a comment
marker in a user message or tool result as suppressed with reason comment, so
it does not gate, until the same rule matches the same value on a live line or
a true\-positive baseline label confirms it. A finding from those four rules
is one severity step lower when its key name carries a test word, a file\-read
tool returned it from a test directory, or a write, or an edit whose path
field is serialized first, targets an env template (.env.example,
\&.env.template, .env.sample, .env.dist; on a host that records the tool input
as flattened key\-value lines, which the JSON\-argument hosts do not); the
lower step never hides it, and the finding also reports confidence low and
likely test value. The same four rules report confidence low, with the
severity untouched, when the field name immediately before the value is a form
token (csrf, xsrf, authenticity_token, nonce and _token spellings) or the
match came back from a web\-fetch tool and from nowhere else in the session;
nothing is removed by either. A provider secret\-key rule reports high on its
own, without source promotion. The forty\-one rules that shared one delimiter
group (the Anthropic, OpenAI, Stripe, GCP, npm, Grafana and Hugging Face keys
among them) match a key whatever delimiter follows it, a delimiter being any
character outside the shared key alphabet of letters, digits, underscore,
plus, slash, equals and hyphen (a comma, a closing parenthesis, a period or a
space as much as a quote); generic\-api\-key, whose value class includes the
period, ends only at whitespace, a quote, a backtick or one of ; , ) ] } < >
|, so a value with ! or & in it is matched whole or not at all, and the JWT
and SendGrid rules refuse a dotted continuation because their alphabets
include the dot, with the captured value ending at the key; a rule with its
own delimiter shape, such as the Postman or Vault token, is unchanged. The
by\-severity line names how many critical findings are attack chains or
behaviors with no secret value. A dynamic\-payload exfiltration that names at
least one URL and whose every named destination is a loopback literal
(localhost, 127.x.x.x in canonical octets, ::1, 0.0.0.0) reports high instead
of critical, with a description saying so, because a loopback address in the
command does not prove the data stayed on the machine; a proxy, DNS, interface
or connection\-override option or a proxy variable on the command line that
names a host outside the loopback set, a config variable, a unix socket or URL
file, a command substitution that is not a plain local read, an unknown
command in the pipeline, or a destination it cannot read as a loopback literal
keeps it critical. The text findings list and the credential recap print one
row per distinct secret value per session file (a keyword\-prefixed rule such
as cloudflare\-api\-key captures its keyword with the value, so it stands
beside a permissive match on the same value rather than grouping with it), the
row carrying the strongest finding (an active finding before a suppressed one,
then the highest severity, then the highest confidence, then the most specific
rule; an identifier value such as a package or variable name is never grouped
or counted) and naming the others after also matched; every incident stays in
the machine formats, where the primary carries alsoMatched, a member carries
groupedUnder, and the top\-level distinctSecretValues counts the primaries
(one per distinct value per session file, so a value leaked in five sessions
counts five) beside the unchanged incident list.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file in addition to the bundled rules. A rule pattern
whose compiled form exceeds 16 KiB is refused like any pattern that fails to
compile, so skarn exits 6 without scanning; an allowlist pattern over that
bound is skipped, so its suppression does not apply.
.TP
\fB\-\-ai\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load an AI\-specific rules file. A rule pattern whose compiled form exceeds 16
KiB is refused like any pattern that fails to compile, so skarn exits 6
without scanning; an allowlist pattern over that bound is skipped, so its
suppression does not apply.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ai\-rules\fR
Disable the AI\-specific rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-default\-rules\fR
Disable the bundled detection rules.
.TP
\fB\-\-feed\fR \fIpath\fR
Apply a signed maintained\-feed bundle over the baseline rules. A feed rule
whose compiled pattern exceeds 16 KiB is skipped with a warning, as is any
feed rule that fails to compile; a rule of the same id that is already loaded
stays in force. Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-update\-rules\fR
Fetch and verify the latest feed from the subscriber channel, then scan.
Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-feed\-url\fR \fIurl\fR
Feed channel URL for \-\-update\-rules; falls back to $SKARN_FEED_URL. It must
be https, with plain http accepted only against loopback, and must carry no
userinfo. Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Disable all network access (the maintained\-feed fetch).
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Report only findings at or above this severity. One of: low, medium, high,
critical. The default is medium.
.TP
\fB\-\-canary\-file\fR \fIpath\fR
File of planted canary tokens, one per line; a match is a proven breach.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\-packages\fR
Flag typosquatted or URL\-sourced package installs found in sessions
(offline).
.TP
\fB\-\-check\-code\fR
Flag insecure code written by the assistant during the session.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Scan window in hours; 0 means no time limit. The default is 720.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to one assistant's sessions. If this machine has no tool by
that name, skarn exits 6 rather than scanning nothing. A custom tool declared
in ~/.skarn.json is accepted by its configured name. One of: claude, gemini,
codex, cursor, copilot, kimi, grok, grokbot, antigravity, opencode, cline,
copilot\-code.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict the scan to matching projects: a comma\-separated list of names or
paths, matched case\-insensitively against a session's project display name,
its canonical root path, or a parent directory of that root. A session read
from a Compliance API export carries no project, so any \-\-project value
excludes it. On a \-\-project run the json reach object of each finding also
lists, as projectNames, the masked names of the projects the finding reached.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Scan a single session by id.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
Output format. ndjson is an ECS\-shaped event stream, one redacted finding per
line, for SIEM ingest. NDJSON\-CONTRACT.md is the field contract for the
ndjson stream. Every ndjson line carries host.name, skarn.identity, skarn.org
and skarn.scanner_user when each is known; skarn.scanner_user is the operating
system account that ran the scan, written as **** when it contains @. Finding
and coverage lines name their assistant as skarn.cli, the summary line lists
the assistants read as skarn.clis, and a line read from a Compliance API
export carries skarn.subject_host set to export. json carries on each incident
a fingerprint equal to the ndjson skarn.fingerprint and, when the incident
belongs to an attack chain, a chainId equal to skarn.chain_id, the first chain
the incident joined. A finding read from a Compliance API export carries the
user id as user.id and the workspace as skarn.workspace_id, both as recorded,
and the email address as user.email fixed to **** when the session carries one
and omitted when it does not, so the mask says an address exists and is
withheld rather than that none was recorded. sarif (partialFingerprints),
ndjson (skarn.fingerprint), evidence, and the json cross\-session chains
(fingerprint and exfilFingerprint) carry the finding fingerprint: the sha256
of the rule id, a NUL byte, and the lowercase hex sha256 of the
ASCII\-lowercased secret. That hash is deterministic and unkeyed, so a
guessable secret is recoverable from it by candidate testing wherever the
output travels. evidence is a redacted Markdown evidence pack for a product
technical file, and the one value that requires a Pro license. Every finding
carries two derived fields: a confidence of high, medium or low, and a flag
that marks a likely test value. text ends each finding line with [confidence
<level>] and adds [likely test value] when the flag is set. json and the sarif
result properties carry confidence and likelyTestValue. ndjson carries
skarn.confidence and skarn.likely_test_value. Confidence is how likely the
match is a live, usable credential after context penalties: a distinctive or
checksummed format, a value that passes the rule's randomness threshold, by a
wide margin for the four keyword\-assignment rules, and use of the value in a
tool call raise it, while a non\-production context, a sighting only in an
assistant message, a test context and a commented line lower it. A finding
with no credential value, such as an attack pattern or a risky command, is
high when it is detected at critical severity or is part of an attack chain
within its session, and medium otherwise; a later non\-production downrank of
its severity does not change its confidence. It is not an exposure ranking,
and neither field changes severity, the risk score or the exit code; the
evidence pack carries neither. Every finding also carries a reach, counted
over the whole run for the same rule and the same value: the distinct sessions
it appeared in, where a session is its assistant, its session store and its
session id; the distinct assistants and projects; the attack chains its
incidents joined; how many of its occurrences there were and how many of them
a baseline or a commented line suppressed; and an execution of executed,
written or unknown. executed means the content sits inside the command
argument of a shell call the agent issued, and says nothing about whether the
command succeeded; written means the agent wrote it into a file that nothing
in the session ran; unknown covers everything else, a file that a later shell
call ran included, because the transcript cannot show that the file still held
the content when it ran. text prints an indented Reach: line under a finding
whose reach spans more than one session, joins an attack chain, names an
execution or counts a suppressed occurrence. json carries a reach object with
sessions, assistants, assistantNames, projects, chains, findings,
suppressedFindings and execution, and adds projectNames only on a \-\-project
run. Reach changes no severity, risk score, exit code or triage order, and
ndjson, sarif, evidence and the \-\-drop artifact carry none. The text format
opens with an Act on this first section: at most five findings that carry a
matched value, one row per value, ranked by a triggered canary first, then
confidence, use of the value in a tool call, a high\-value issuer (GitHub,
AWS, GCP, Azure, a Stripe live key, a private key or a database URI),
membership of an attack chain and the most recent sighting. Each row states
the redacted value and those facts, and a line below the rows counts the
values left out as a likely test value, low confidence or suppressed. json,
sarif, ndjson and evidence carry no section. The ndjson stream also carries
one skarn.inventory line directly after the summary, the record skarn
inventory prints, so check \-\-format ndjson reads the assistant configuration
files skarn vet reads, the guard configuration layers and the installed
license, read\-only; if that collection fails the line carries event.outcome
failure, stderr names it, and it counts as a scan\-infrastructure gap that
\-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error turns into exit 6. Rules that check loads through
\-\-feed or \-\-update\-rules do not reach the inventory line; a \-\-rules
pack does. Any other verdict exit takes precedence over that 6, so read
event.outcome on the line to learn whether the collection completed. A finding
whose matched value was seen inside a tool result that carried a Microsoft
sensitivity label shape is marked with skarn.sensitivity_label.observed and
skarn.sensitivity_label.shapes in ndjson and with sensitivityLabelObserved and
sensitivityLabelShapes in json. The ndjson summary line
(skarn.sensitivity_label.tool_results and skarn.sensitivity_label.sessions),
the json top\-level counters sensitivityLabelToolResults and
sensitivityLabelSessions, and one text line count the tool results that
carried a label shape and the sessions that held them. No label name or label
id is written, and the observation changes neither the verdict nor the exit
code. One of: text, json, sarif, ndjson, evidence. The default is text.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Verbose output.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output.
.TP
\fB\-\-policy\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a declarative TOML policy file: fail thresholds, required/forbidden
rules, baseline enforcement, and the output profile. The \-\-fail\-on\-* and
\-\-profile flags override it. A file skarn cannot read or parse exits 4
rather than falling back to the embedded default, which gates nothing.
Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-profile\fR \fIpreset\fR
Output profile. enterprise keeps high\-confidence findings and dedups them.
One of: enterprise. Requires an Enterprise license.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Exit 1 if any finding is at or above this severity. One of: low, medium, high,
critical.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-risk\fR \fI0\-100\fR
Exit 2 if the session risk score exceeds this threshold.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error\fR
Fail closed with exit 6 when the scan was incomplete: a tool source failed
discovery, a session store that exists could not be read, a session failed to
parse, a session held a record that could not be parsed (a malformed line, or
a line cut at the line limit, 64 MiB of bytes or 8 Mi structural tokens
outside strings, which then never parses, and in a Gemini CLI chat after such
a line also a patch record whose message or tool call the lost line held; the
first 1 MiB of its bytes is scanned as raw text, labelled raw record, so the
detectors still run over those bytes, and the record stays a coverage gap
because its role, tool and the rest of its content are unknown; a session
keeps at most 256 such records or 16 MiB of them, and a refused record past
that budget is counted and not scanned), a session had truncated or compacted
transcript content so it no longer holds the whole conversation (the assistant
compacted it before the scan, or a compliance transcript export carried a
truncated tool block, a truncated text block, or a compaction marker), a
compliance transcript export withheld a turn as unavailable or carried a
client\-asserted turn the feed did not verify, or the scan read no sessions.
Truncated or compacted content counts sessions, because a cut block is a
partial turn and nobody can count what a compaction removed; withheld and
unverified content counts turns, because the export names each one. Every
truncated tool block counts its session, including a block returned as a
placeholder instead of cut text, so a coverage gap is over\-reported rather
than missed. A record that could not be parsed counts itself, and the JSON and
NDJSON output name each session file that dropped one. An unterminated last
line that does not parse but begins a JSON object (its first character after
leading whitespace is an opening brace), in an uncompressed session file
modified within the last 5 minutes, is a write in progress and is not counted;
the same line in an older or compressed file is counted, and so is an
unterminated last line that does not parse and does not begin a JSON object. A
string value longer than 8 MiB inside a record (a pasted image stored inline,
a huge tool result) is shortened to a marker before the record is parsed, so
the record and every other value in it are scanned as usual; the scan counts
such records as shortened in its JSON, SARIF, NDJSON and assess outputs, names
each session file that holds one beside the files that dropped a record, and
counts them as a coverage gap, because the bytes past the cap were never
scanned. A Grok prompt log prompt is cut at the same 8 MiB without a marker
and counted the same way on its session. A value nested deeper than 64 levels
inside a tool input, a tool result or another structured part of a session is
not read, because the parser stops descending there so hostile nesting cannot
exhaust its stack; the scan counts each such value in its JSON, SARIF, NDJSON
and assess outputs, names each session file that holds one, and counts it as a
coverage gap, so a session whose only credential sits past that depth is never
reported clean, unless the text the parser builds from the tool input or
result that holds the value has already reached its size limit. The shortened
count and then the depth count end the coverage sentence wherever that
sentence is printed. By default a partial scan still reports, and an empty
scan still warns on stderr.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIpath\fR
Suppress findings recorded in this baseline, so the scan fails only on new
ones. A single file is free; a directory unions every .json baseline inside
it, an org\-shared accepted set, and requires a Pro license. That union leaves
out any fingerprint a member labelled a true positive. Without this flag skarn
applies ~/.config/skarn/baseline.json when it exists, for suppression only;
the \-\-baseline\-create and \-\-baseline\-merge targets stay explicit.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\-create\fR
Write the current findings to the \-\-baseline file as the accepted set, then
exit. Under \-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error an incomplete scan is refused instead:
the run exits 6 and leaves the existing file as it was. The file is written
atomically under the baseline lock and is readable by its owner only on macOS
and Linux; a symlink at the path is replaced by the new file. When the sibling
\&.lock file cannot be opened or created the write is refused and an existing
file is left as it was.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\-merge\fR \fIfile\fR
Consolidate the \-\-baseline input (a file or a directory of per\-member
baselines) into one distributable file and exit. The output is written
atomically under the baseline lock, readable by its owner only on macOS and
Linux, and the input is read inside that same lock, so an in\-place merge
cannot lose a concurrent accept; a symlink at the output path is replaced.
When the sibling .lock file cannot be opened or created the merge is refused
and an existing output file is left as it was. Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-audit\-log\fR \fIfile\fR
Append a hash\-chained record of this scan (timestamp, policy, finding counts,
verdict; no secrets) to this local log. The chain detects in\-place edits and
reordering of recorded history. A new log is created readable by its owner
only on macOS and Linux and an existing log keeps its mode; on those systems a
symlink at the log path is refused with a warning naming AuditLogSymlink, and
the scan result and exit code are unchanged. Requires a Pro license.
.TP
\fB\-\-audit\-verify\fR \fIfile\fR
Verify an audit log's hash chain, then exit without scanning. A broken chain
exits 1. A log that exists but could not be read exits 6: a permission
failure, a directory, or a log past the read cap. An absent log is a valid
empty chain and exits 0. Needs no license.
.TP
\fB\-\-drop\fR \fIdir\fR
After the scan, write a self\-describing drop artifact to
<dir>/<identity>/<runId>.json: identity, org, host, run id, scan window, the
\-\-project scope it declares, and the redacted summary counts. The counts
cover the declared scope, not the machine, so the session count is the number
that passed \-\-project and \-\-session. Scope is recorded twice:
scope.projects is the \-\-project needles verbatim, often a path, and
scope.resolved is the project names those needles matched. Content is
attributed against scope.resolved, so a run that declared no scope resolves
nothing and therefore names no project at all. The assistant list and the
last\-activity timestamp describe the sessions the scan read inside that
scope, not the ones that produced a finding. The guard mode comes from the
same wired\-hook walk doctor performs, which only this flag pays for; an
absent guard mode means it could not be resolved, not that no hook is wired.
The optional lastDeny records the newest deny the guard enforced before
execution on this machine, inside the \-\-hours window (0 means the whole log)
and, with \-\-project, only for a project the scan itself resolved in
scope.resolved, using the same resolver for aliases and worktrees. An unscoped
run accepts any working directory and records an empty project. Skarn reads
the logs skarn guard report reads with no flags: $SKARN_GUARD_LOG when set,
otherwise guard\-<agent>\-audit.jsonl under $XDG_CACHE_HOME/skarn or
~/.cache/skarn; a log configured elsewhere with skarn setup \-\-log <path>
counts only when $SKARN_GUARD_LOG names it. Post\-execution blocks such as
PostToolUse, audit\-mode would\-denies, events the host cannot enforce, and a
Copilot CLI permissionRequest deny, which a later hook can override, never
count. A secret preview uses only the mask the hook wrote at the redaction
chokepoint, never a raw value; the working directory, session id and
fingerprint never enter lastDeny. A record is also ineligible when it does not
record enforceable as true, when the run sets \-\-project and the record's
working directory is not an absolute path, when its rule id or tool name uses
anything other than letters, digits, dots, hyphens and underscores, or when a
field carries a control byte or a credential\-shaped value. An absent lastDeny
means no readable log held an eligible deny; missing, unreadable or corrupt
logs do not stop the drop write. The write is atomic, so a second run in the
same window adds a file rather than overwriting one. check \-\-drop writes
only drop schema version 4; fleet aggregate reads the current and previous
drop schemas, 4 and 3, each under its own key set, and refuses every other
version. The aggregator refuses a schema 3 drop carrying lastDeny by name, and
refuses a schema 4 drop whose lastDeny fails the same field screens, names an
event that is not a pre\-execution event, names a project outside the drop's
resolved scope, or carries an at outside the scan window that ends at the
drop's generatedAt. Upgrade the host running fleet aggregate first, then the
reporting machines, and leave schema 3 drops in the collection directory; an
older aggregator refuses a schema 4 drop and the whole run. Skarn does not
transmit it; moving the directory is the organization's job. Identity comes
from SKARN_IDENTITY, falling back to a hostname\-derived tag with a warning.
.TP
\fB\-\-product\fR \fIname\fR
Release lineage for \-\-format evidence: the product this scan's sessions
changed. Without lineage, the evidence pack states that it cannot be attached
to a specific technical file.
.TP
\fB\-\-product\-version\fR \fIver\fR
Release lineage for \-\-format evidence: the product version the pack
describes.
.TP
\fB\-\-build\-id\fR \fIid\fR
Release lineage for \-\-format evidence: the commit or build identifier the
pack describes.
.TP
\fB\-\-sbom\-ref\fR \fIref\fR
Release lineage for \-\-format evidence: a reference to the product's declared
SBOM, recorded as supplied (control characters masked). Skarn neither parses
nor fetches it.
.TP
\fB\-\-sbom\fR \fIpath\fR
Reconcile production dependencies the sessions added to a manifest against
this declared SBOM, in CycloneDX or SPDX 2.x JSON format. Each undeclared
addition is reported as low\-severity workflow\-drift evidence, so pair it
with \-\-severity low. Unlike \-\-sbom\-ref, skarn reads and parses the file;
an unreadable or malformed one fails the scan.
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to matching users: a comma\-separated list of user ids or
email addresses, where an id matches exactly and an email address matches
case\-insensitively. Only a session read from a Compliance API export carries
either, so on a machine without an export, or under a registered license below
Enterprise, every \-\-user value matches nothing and the report is empty at
exit 0. The drop artifact and the evidence pack do not record a user
narrowing, so a narrowed run reads like a whole\-corpus one there.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
Clean, or informational output only. For mcp: the client closed stdin, which
is the normal shutdown.
.TP
\fB1\fR
A finding at or above the \-\-fail\-on\-severity threshold was reported. For
setup and guard \-\-self\-test: a target was refused or a verification failed.
For doctor: at least one check FAILED (a warning never exits nonzero), or an
unknown option was passed, which is refused before any check runs. For
restore: the recorded working directory or session id could not be rendered
safely, or the working directory carries a detected secret, so the resume
command cannot be printed, nothing was printed to stdout and the refusal is
named on stderr. Also: the first\-run EULA prompt was declined, so the invoked
operation was not performed. For mcp: an unknown argument was passed, which is
refused before the protocol loop starts. For license: an install or a renewal
was refused or did not complete, for example because an argument was invalid,
a license did not verify, a replacement was declined, there was no license to
renew, the renewal endpoint was refused or could not be reached, or a file
could not be read or written. A failure before the new license is written
leaves the installed license as it was.
.TP
\fB2\fR
The session risk score exceeded the \-\-fail\-on\-risk threshold. For setup:
no terminal to prompt on and no \-\-yes; the exact non\-interactive command is
printed. For license: the license service declined the renewal that license
renew asked for, and nothing was written.
.TP
\fB3\fR
A canary token was triggered (a proven breach). This overrides every other
gate. For license: license renew refused the license the service returned,
because it did not verify, could not be used (revoked or expired, for
example), or did not supersede the license being renewed or the one already at
the destination, so nothing was written.
.TP
\fB4\fR
A policy precondition was not met: a required rule is missing, the policy
requires a baseline and none was supplied or the one supplied did not load, or
the \-\-policy file itself could not be read or parsed. A policy that never
loaded is never quietly replaced by the embedded default, which gates nothing.
.TP
\fB5\fR
A requested paid feature is not covered by the active license (a Pro or
Enterprise flag without a license that covers it). Refused before any scan.
With no usable license at all, check exits 7 before this gate is consulted.
.TP
\fB6\fR
The scan could not complete: session discovery or the scan itself failed,
including a home directory that could not be resolved so no session store was
ever located, or the detection rules could not be loaded so no scan was ever
defined. Under \-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error an otherwise\-complete run exits 6 as
well whenever coverage was partial: a tool source dropped out of discovery, a
session store that exists could not be read, a session's parse failed, a
session held a record that could not be parsed (in a Gemini CLI chat after
such a record, also a patch record whose target the lost line held) and so was
scanned only as raw text, up to its first 1 MiB and within a per\-session
budget of 256 such records or 16 MiB, past which the record was not scanned at
all (an unterminated last line that does not parse but begins a JSON object,
in an uncompressed file modified within the last 5 minutes, is a write in
progress and is exempt), a session held a record with a string value over 8
MiB that was shortened before parsing and so was scanned without the rest of
that value, a session held a value nested deeper than 64 levels that was not
scanned, the assistant compacted a session before the scan so its transcript
no longer holds the whole conversation, or the run read no sessions at all.
That verdict is computed BEFORE the report is rendered, so the SARIF
invocation exit code, the evidence pack and the audit record all carry 6
rather than describing the run as clean and then exiting nonzero. A
\-\-baseline\-create run is refused on the same condition before it writes
anything, so an incomplete scan is never recorded as the accepted set and the
previous baseline survives. For check \-\-audit\-verify: the audit log exists
but could not be read, so the hash chain was never examined (an absent log is
a valid empty chain and exits 0). For vet: the home directory could not be
resolved (so the configuration was never located at all), or a config file
that exists was not read \- it could not be read, or it was skipped because it
is not that assistant's live configuration \- so the configuration was only
partly seen. For doctor: the home directory could not be resolved, so every
per\-assistant check would have read an empty machine \- a diagnostic that
could not be produced is never reported as a healthy one. Fail\-closed \- the
result is not trustworthy, so it is never reported as clean.
.TP
\fB7\fR
No usable license is present \- none was found via SKARN_LICENSE,
SKARN_LICENSE_FILE, or the installed file \- so check, baseline audit, fleet
aggregate or fleet readout refused before scanning or reading a file. The free
license is issued at https://getskarn.com/free after a quick email
confirmation and is verified offline. Also used when a license IS present but
cannot be used at all (revoked, tampered, or signed by an untrusted key).
assess, baseline accept, guard accept, the recall commands, guard, setup,
doctor, vet, mcp, license, taxonomies and check \-\-audit\-verify never exit
7.
.SH SECURITY
Skarn scans locally and makes no network connection by default. Secrets are
redacted in check's reports, in everything the recall commands print (search,
recent, messages, stats, cmds, tools, mcps, restore, export), in the ndjson
stream, the evidence pack, the artifacts written by \-\-drop, serve and
assess, the baseline files, the mcp tools, the guard, and every serve API
route that returns session content. No flag lifts the mask.
.PP
Two commands transmit anything off the machine, and only when you type them.
skarn check \-\-update\-rules fetches a signed feed bundle from the subscriber
channel, presenting $SKARN_FEED_TOKEN as a bearer credential; \-\-feed\-url
names that channel when $SKARN_FEED_URL does not. The feed is off by default
and is disabled by \-\-offline. skarn license renew posts the installed
license token to the license service to receive a re\-signed one. Both refuse
any endpoint that is not https, or http against loopback, and any endpoint
carrying userinfo. No session content, no finding, and no scanned secret ever
leaves the machine through a connection skarn makes. skarn mcp makes no
network connection; the results it hands to the connected MCP client (redacted
findings by default, redacted session content only with \-\-enable\-recall)
are forwarded by that client to its model provider \- that egress is the
client's, and it happens only when you configure the server.
.PP
skarn vet reads the AI assistant configuration files listed under FILES
read\-only: it opens each one for reading, never writes, rewrites, or removes
one, and makes no network call. It reports what it finds and leaves every
decision about the configuration to you. \-\-offline is accepted for parity
and changes nothing.
.PP
skarn mcp reads the same session stores as assess and the same configuration
files as vet, read\-only and with no network call, and answers only the AI
coding assistant that spawned it over stdin and stdout. Its results are the
redacted scan report, the masked vet report, and session metadata carrying
neither message text nor any label authored inside a transcript; with
\-\-enable\-recall the two content tools add session text and labels with
every detected secret masked, and the search response carries no echo of the
query or of the project filter. Because the assistant receives those results,
they travel wherever that assistant sends its context, so treat them as
leaving the machine even though skarn itself opens no socket.
